# @boundary runs
# @boundary-pending BUG-236  `/` uses @divTrunc but `%` uses @mod; identity fails
#
# BOUNDARY: integer division and modulo with NEGATIVE operands.
#
# Under-specified today: QUICKSTART says only "use `%` for modulo" and never states
# which way division truncates. That silence is the reason to probe it — two
# defensible conventions exist and they disagree on every negative case:
#
#   truncate toward zero (C, Zig @divTrunc):  -7 / 2 == -3,  -7 % 2 == -1
#   floor toward -inf    (Python, @divFloor): -7 / 2 == -4,  -7 % 2 ==  1
#
# Intent asserted TRUNCATION TOWARD ZERO for both. Division does exactly that. MODULO
# DOES NOT -- it floors -- so the two operators disagree and the identity
# `(a/b)*b + (a%b) == a` is FALSE. That is BUG-236, found by this probe, and the
# .expected file below now records the BROKEN behaviour so the gate stays honest.
# When BUG-236 is fixed this probe FAILS: rewrite modNegPos to -1, modPosNeg to 1,
# identity to true, and drop the pending line.
# Its own file: signed division is also the row most likely not to compile at all, as
# Zig REFUSES a bare `/` on signed integers precisely because the convention is
# ambiguous, and requires @divTrunc/@divFloor to be named explicitly.
#
# Whatever the answer is, it should be WRITTEN DOWN — that is the finding either way.
def main()
    print("posPos=${7 / 2}")
    print("negPos=${(0 - 7) / 2}")
    print("posNeg=${7 / (0 - 2)}")
    print("negNeg=${(0 - 7) / (0 - 2)}")

    print("modPosPos=${7 % 2}")
    print("modNegPos=${(0 - 7) % 2}")
    print("modPosNeg=${7 % (0 - 2)}")

    # Exact division of a negative leaves no remainder either way.
    print("exactNeg=${(0 - 6) / 2}")
    print("exactNegMod=${(0 - 6) % 2}")

    # The identity that must hold whichever convention is chosen.
    var a = 0 - 7
    var b = 2
    print("identity=${(a / b) * b + (a % b) == a}")
